WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.910
- So according to the
Biden administration,

00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:04.020
the war in Afghanistan is over.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.430
But what legally allowed
that war to happen

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:08.900
in the first place, and
could it happen again?

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.530
Only Congress has the
power to declare war.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:14.500
It's right there in Article
I of the Constitution.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:16.870
So how is it that presidents
basically seem to have

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:19.320
unilateral ability to declare
war whenever they want?

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.910
In this particular case
we're talking about

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.710
the authorization for use
of military force or AUMF.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.770
The 2001 AUMF allowed the president

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:26.910
to go to war in Afghanistan,

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:29.903
and there were also AUMFs in 1991 and 2002

00:00:29.903 --> 00:00:32.240
that allowed the president
to go to war in Iraq.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.650
Those are still on the books,

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:35.600
which leads many to say that perhaps now

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.770
we should remove them
since those wars are over

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:39.870
and we don't want presidents

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:42.770
extending those AUMFs to
go to war in other places.

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:43.940
This is part of a longstanding battle

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.560
between Congress and the
president over who can declare

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:47.850
and continue to go to war.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:50.220
It's absolutely not a settled
constitutional question,

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.440
but the ending of the war in Afghanistan

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.740
has raised these questions once again.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:57.740
So, perhaps this time maybe
something will be done about it.

